WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
Specialization and customization points are powerful extension mechanisms.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:16.120
Explicit specializations, partial specializations, traits, policy classes, tag dispatch, and customization point objects can all change behavior based on type.

3
00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:22.060
That is useful for performance and integration, but it also changes the security review surface.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:31.060
A specialization should not silently bypass validation, change ownership, relax safety checks, or alter policy in a way that reviewers cannot see.

5
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:38.360
If a trait says a type is safe for a fast path, the codebase needs confidence that the trait is accurate and maintained.

6
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.760
Extension points should have documented responsibilities.

7
00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:45.060
A policy type may need to preserve invariants.

8
00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.200
A serializer may need to reject malformed data.

9
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.860
A comparator may need to implement a stable ordering.

10
00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.120
A custom allocator may need to respect lifetime and cleanup expectations.

11
00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.260
Those responsibilities are part of the API contract.

12
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:03.580
Governance matters because customization can appear far from the generic code that uses it.

13
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.080
Security-relevant specializations should receive the same attention as direct changes to the main implementation.

14
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:18.340
Reviewers should ask what behavior changes, what invariants still hold, and which tests cover the specialized path.

15
00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.020
Good customization boundaries are explicit.

16
00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:26.480
They let teams extend generic code without letting hidden policy changes spread unnoticed through the system.

17
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.320
Ownership of extension points should be clear as well.

18
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:39.740
If many teams can add specializations, the project needs naming, testing, and review expectations so policy changes are not scattered silently across the codebase.
